  "interpretation": "# What the usage numbers tell us\n\n## What changed\n\nOver the last 90 days, 3,127 people took 1,284,547 actions, and 59.51% came back again. Weekly use fell from 830 people in mid-August to 323 in late September, then rose to 853 in the week of October 18.\n\nIn the latest 14 days, people rose 102.61% and actions rose 63.21%, but estimated work rose only 12.29%. This may suggest many newcomers starting with lighter tasks. Upgrades moved fast: release 2.0.8 reached 56.28% of weekly people in late September, and 2.5.5 already reached 22.04% in the newest partial week.\n\n## How people use it\n\nReading and writing cells is the core, at 63.43% of actions. The most common actions were reading cell values (293,680) and writing them (232,884). Share of work is an estimate built from fixed effort levels: quick reads count 1, everyday edits count 3, and heavy data work counts 10. It is not time or speed. By this estimate, macros (small automation programs) hold 16.54% of work but 7.88% of actions, and data imports hold 10.59% versus 3.91%. Fewer people do these advanced tasks, but each counts as heavier work.\n\nAI assistant sessions had a typical size of 10 actions, and 68.92% touched more than one area. Cell work and sheet tasks appeared together in 57.03% of sessions. Of 2,698 new people, 51.48% returned after a week and 38.32% after three weeks. Most first advanced use happens on day one, such as 79.29% for macros. Workdays averaged 29,941 actions versus 14,170 on weekends. Typical waits ranged from 0.08 seconds for sheet tasks to 1.59 seconds for data imports. Waits depend on workbook size and computer, so they are not a quality score.\n\nSince September 28, a shorter window, the AI assistant group had 3,200 people and the command line group 315. These are different people. Command line users averaged 667.1 actions each versus 214.2, and leaned more on cell work (78.46% versus 59.48%). This may suggest repeated, scripted jobs.\n\n## Where people get it\n\nDownloads count downloads, not people. One person can download several times, and updates and automated installs add to the totals. The channels overlap, so they should not be added together. GitHub release files lead with 27,381 and VS Code installs reach 9,397, both counted since each channel started. Over the last 12 months, npm shows 6,147 for the AI assistant server and 3,834 for the command line. Weekly npm downloads peaked at 5,178 in the week of October 4, then fell to 1,639.\n\nRelease 2.4.1 was downloaded most, with 1,000, followed by 2.5.0 with 784. Weekly gains grew from 1,794 in the week of October 12 to 1,994 in the week of October 19, mostly from GitHub release files.\n\n## What we will improve\n\nThese are plans, not findings. We will make first steps easier for newcomers, since recent growth leans toward lighter work. We will improve guides for macros and data imports, which many people try on their first day. We will keep watching whether new people come back after three weeks.",
